 {"id":"CF-371","title":"Fleet ops census backup check still flags a MOCK database package: heading reports a P1 store gap whose only trigger is packages/mock-db/migrations (residual of CF-370)","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":1,"reasoning":"Measured by the fleet-ops-check tick fleet-ops-check-2026-11-06-00-02-50 (census run AFTER the CF-370 backup-heuristic patch landed live: `cd ~/.hermes/scripts && git apply --check --reverse ~/code-factory/patches/fleet-ops-backup-heuristic.patch` exits 0, so the patched build is what produced these numbers).\n\n`python3 ~/.hermes/scripts/fleet_ops_check.py --json` still reports check=backup P1 on 7 families. Five are genuine (asce, crier, dexdat-core, helios, off-by-one - filed or already filed on their own boards). heading is not: its ONLY surviving trigger is `packages/mock-db/migrations/001_initial_schema.sql`, and packages/mock-db/package.json is literally `{\"name\": \"@heading/mock-db\", \"private\": true, \"description\": \"Mock SQLite database for HEADING travel system\"}`. Every importer is demo/test support - apps/api/src/demo/seed-assert.ts, apps/api/src/orchestrator/mock-providers.ts, apps/api/src/orchestrator/airport-routes.ts, apps/api/tests/**. The real API has no store of its own: apps/api/src has no postgres/sqlite DSN beyond apps/api/src/routes/integrations.ts, and docker-compose.yml defines no datastore service (db-preflight is a node:22-alpine script runner).\n\nSo a MOCK datastore counts as an owned production datastore, and heading's -ops lane would have to re-derive that refutation every tick forever - the exact silent-re-reporting class CF-370 was filed to end. This row is the residual arm of the same check, not a second row about the same defect: CF-370 fixed harness bookkeeping and basename matching, and this fixes test-support packages.","acceptance_criteria":"1. The census does not treat a migrations/ or schema.sql path under a test-support package as a production store: a path component matching mock|mock-db|fixture|fixtures|fake|stub|testdb counts only when it is NOT the sole store evidence for the family. Concretely, after the change `python3 ~/.hermes/scripts/fleet_ops_check.py --project heading --json` reports no check=backup gap, while `--project asce`, `--project crier`, `--project dexdat-core`, `--project helios` and `--project off-by-one` still do (they own real stores with no backup path).\n2. Negative control A: a fixture tree containing only packages/mock-db/migrations/001.sql reports NO backup gap.\n3. Negative control B (the arm that must keep the check alive): a fixture tree containing packages/db/migrations/001.sql - a non-mock package - still reports check=backup P1.\n4. Negative control C: a fixture tree containing BOTH packages/mock-db/migrations/001.sql AND a real migrations/001.sql at the root still reports P1 (the mock exclusion cannot swallow a genuine store beside it).\n5. Durability: the delta ships as patches/<name>.patch beside patches/<name>.test.py (argv[1] = target script, default ~/.hermes/scripts/fleet_ops_check.py), `git apply --check` passes forward on the recorded pristine copy and `--reverse` on the live patched file, and the test asserts arms 1-4.","created_at":"2026-11-06T00:40:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T00:40:00Z","detail":{}}
 {"id":"CF-372","title":"install-deepseek.sh and install-pi-agent.sh: contract-conforming adapters so the conformance vars leg can be green for every matrix row","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-06T00:49Z while closing out CF-229. `tests/harness-conformance.sh --vars-only` reports 6 pass, 2 fail, 0 skip: deepseek:vars FAIL \"no adapter at scripts/harness/install-deepseek.sh — no installer to hold to the §5 contract\" and pi-agent:vars FAIL \"no adapter at scripts/harness/install-pi-agent.sh\". The conformance matrix lists seven harness rows; only four adapters exist under scripts/harness (hermes, claude-code, codex, opencode), and install-opencode.sh doubles for the opencode-v2 row. So the vars leg CF-229 built can never be green for every matrix row until the two missing adapters exist, and that is a separate deliverable from the contract itself. Both rows were already FAIL on check/apply/smoke before CF-229 landed, so this row is not a regression — it is an unowned gap that CF-229 exposed. pi-agent has an auth surface on this host (~/.pi/agent/auth.json names deepseek,openrouter, all reporting invalid) and no binary on PATH; deepseek has neither. The adapters must therefore be honest stubs in the shape install-opencode.sh already uses: they validate the contract and refuse --apply with a named reason rather than pretending to install something unmeasured.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/harness/install-deepseek.sh and scripts/harness/install-pi-agent.sh exist, source scripts/harness/harness-vars.sh rather than re-implementing §5, accept the full declared set as both a flag and a CF_* environment variable, document every declared name in --help, and refuse a credential VALUE by name — proven by tests/harness/vars-contract-test.sh passing for all six adapters it covers.\n2. tests/harness-conformance.sh --vars-only exits 0 with eight pass, zero fail, zero skip, naming deepseek and pi-agent explicitly.\n3. Neither adapter claims support it cannot prove: --apply is refused with a named reason, or the adapter names the ladder it actually ran, and a missing binary reports FAIL naming the binary rather than PASS.\n4. tests/selftest.sh stays PASS (SELFTEST: PASS, EXIT=0) and bash -n is clean on both new files.","created_at":"2026-11-06T00:50:33Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T01:03:51Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"ad62fa7f3a5df02f34c22145a848b06f91ddcbb1a650ad623203ab72bdc436c8","evidence":[{"run_id":"df7b7412","ts":"2026-11-06T01:03:51Z","tree_fp":"9a50602de8b4b02705a64414a57c7fc9a8c106a0c5c80d76a1bba93eb9e3d1ce","artifacts":["verdict.json"]}]},"commit_hash":"a53d173e15e3f941d14585b0c0c00a2e957ccded","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"CF-372 graded and closed by code-factory-ops tick 2026-11-06-00-39-44. The fix landed as a53d173 (sibling code-factory lane committed 19:59:17 and stopped before closeout); Tier 2 verdict PASS at a53d173; conformance --vars-only 8 pass 0 fail 0 skip naming deepseek and pi-agent; vars-contract 32 pass 0 fail 1 skip; bash -n clean; selftest PASS.","completed_at":"2026-11-06T01:02:58Z"}
 {"id":"CF-373","title":"gitreins task complete is SIGTERMed mid-judge (rc=143) and still flips the record to complete, so the closure has to be recovered by hand","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-06 by tick code-factory-ops-2026-11-06-00-39-44 on base 4edbced. 'gitreins task complete CF-288-OPS1' printed 'Completed: CF-288-OPS1 -> complete', flipped .gitreins/tasks.yaml to complete at 01:34:41Z with its completed_at stamped, then sat in 'Evaluating...' and was terminated by SIGTERM after ~10 minutes (shell rc=143). No verdict directory and no verdict.json were written by that run. The wedge shape is the dangerous part: the lifecycle record says complete while no artifact exists, so any later lane that trusts tasks.yaml closes a row on evidence nobody can open. The same task re-judged with 'task start' + 'judge --async' finished cleanly in 3m39s (01:46:46 -> 01:50:25) and wrote .gitreins/history/2026-11-06/336a8021/verdict.json with passed=true. Two things to establish: what sent SIGTERM (host scope reaper, evaluator cap, or the tool window), and why the record flip precedes the verdict write instead of following it.","acceptance_criteria":"1. The process that terminates a judge mid-run is named from evidence (process census, journal, or reaper log), not guessed, and the finding says whether it is host-wide or specific to this lane. 2. The record flip is either moved after the verdict write, or 'task complete' refuses to leave a task complete when no verdict artifact for that run exists; a fixture proves the refusal. 3. A judge terminated mid-run leaves a recoverable state: a single documented command restores the task and produces a verdict whose run directory resolves, proven once end to end. 4. The row states which of the two read paths a foreman must use to tell 'judged' from 'flipped and abandoned', and tests/selftest.sh or the gitreins self-test covers that distinction. 5. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS and bash docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh prints RESULT: OK at the closing commit.","created_at":"2026-11-06T01:56:47Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T05:15:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"0ed9b67a69929f09823dcf3aa099183d96b979ebdb6adec9278bebd2e95e0328"},"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"completed_at":null,"foreman_note":"SECOND INSTANCE, 2026-11-06T05:0xZ, tick code-factory-2026-11-06-04-01-25, and it sharpens the class rather than repeating it: the record flip is not only caused by an external SIGTERM. 'gitreins task complete CF-245' (a verification-only close with NO source diff) ran tier 1 PASS, then tier 2 to 'Iteration cap (100) reached (100.8 used)' with passed=false and items=[] (verdict run 6cfe3061), leaving tasks.yaml at status=complete with no usable verdict. Re-judged once at GITREINS_MAX_ITERATIONS=250: tier 2 consumed 9,079,161 input tokens, reported compaction #1, and then hung 32 minutes — parent /proc/<pid>/io static across a 12 s sample with zero sockets, which reads exactly like a dead wedge, while ps --ppid showed a LIVE child '/bin/sh -c sleep 29; grep -n \"SELFTEST:\" /tmp/selftest3.log'. That log carried zero SELFTEST lines (27,617 bytes, mtime 23:51) and no test process was running, so the poll could never observe completion and would have consumed all 250 iterations. Classify from the CHILD cmdline, never from parent io alone. The foreman terminated it after 32 minutes; two runs cost ~9M input tokens and 38 minutes of a 2 h lane on a row with no code. Read path this row asks for: a completed task whose run has no verdict.json is 'flipped and abandoned', not 'judged' — .gitreins/history/<today>/<dir>/verdict.json must exist AND its task_id must match AND passed must be true."}
-{"id":"CF-374","title":"master_doc_check.py prints MASTER-DOC: PASS over 0 modules: its default root docs/master no longer exists, so the doc gate cannot fail","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-06 by foreman tick code-factory-2026-11-06-04-01-25 at bb2e296. docs/master does not exist (the Apollo re-arrange moved the master document to docs/handbook), yet python3 scripts/master_doc_check.py with no arguments prints '0 module(s): 0 VERIFIED, 0 error(s), 0 warning(s), 0 fact(s) in the ledger' followed by 'MASTER-DOC: PASS', rc=0. A gate that reports a clean pass over an empty module set cannot fail, so the locator/FACT drift it exists to catch is unobservable: docs/handbook/appendices/units/09-scripts-clone-lane-py.md still pins [scripts/clone_lane.py:1-92] and revision 83b2194 while the file is 130 lines after CF-357. CF-311 and CF-323 cover other master-doc defects; this is the vacuous-pass root defect.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/master_doc_check.py with no arguments on this tree either resolves the live master-doc root and counts its modules (count greater than zero) or exits nonzero naming the missing root; it must never print MASTER-DOC: PASS over 0 modules. 2. A zero-module run is a named FAIL: a fixture whose declared root is absent exits nonzero with a reason naming the root, and a fixture holding one module counts 1 and can still pass. 3. The default root matches the root the Apollo structure actually ships, and the acceptance command re-runs both fixtures from a clean checkout. 4. tests/selftest.sh covers the vacuous case, so the gate is red-proof.","created_at":"2026-11-06T04:25:41Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T09:15:54Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"ea902c77fc916a08f59a773a29f4510c3bf2f51743c6d78e91b6b2b17134e36b"},"commit_hash":"336e7cd9cb3d70665e7d08e5f8c23a7503a14531","guard_result":"SKIP","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"scripts/master_doc_check.py now resolves its module root instead of globbing a lit