 {"id":"CF-375","title":"tier-2 judge on a no-diff verification close: cap INCOMPLETE at 100.8 iterations, then a 32-minute sleep-poll over a log with zero SELFTEST lines, leaving the record complete with no verdict","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-06T05:15:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T05:15:05Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"cf375-iteration-cap-and-never-firing-poll"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-06 by tick code-factory-2026-11-06-04-01-25 while closing CF-245, a verification-only row with NO source diff. Two independent runs of 'gitreins task complete CF-245' failed to produce a usable verdict, and neither failure was about the work. Run 1 (verdict dir .gitreins/history/2026-11-06/6cfe3061) reached tier 2 with 'Cap exceeded: Iteration cap (100) reached (100.8 used)', passed=false, items=[] \u2014 the judge never emitted a single criterion result. Run 2, re-judged at GITREINS_MAX_ITERATIONS=250, consumed 9,079,161 input tokens (largest single prompt 134,859) and logged 'compacting (compaction #1)', then spent 32 minutes in a health poll with child cmdline '/bin/sh -c sleep 29; grep -n \"SELFTEST:\" /tmp/selftest3.log' while /tmp/selftest3.log contained ZERO 'SELFTEST' lines (27,617 bytes, mtime 23:51) and no test process was running: the detector could never fire, and it would have eaten every remaining iteration the same way. The wedge is invisible from the parent, whose /proc/<pid>/io is static across a 12 s sample with zero sockets; only ps --ppid reveals the live child. The foreman terminated run 2, so .gitreins/tasks.yaml reads status=complete for CF-245 with no verdict.json \u2014 CF-373's defect class reached from a different cause (self-inflicted poll loop, not an external SIGTERM). Cost: ~9M input tokens and 38 minutes of a 2 h lane on a row with no code to grade. Distinct from CF-373 (which covers the SIGTERM-to-complete flip and the judged-vs-abandoned read path) because the remedy here is instrument-side: a verification-only criterion must not be able to drive the evaluator into running and polling the whole suite, and an unfireable wait must be detected rather than retried.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Reproduce today's failure on the current tree: the stored verdict run 6cfe3061 is present in the committed history, reads passed=false, carries ZERO criterion items, and its summary names the iteration cap (probe below prints false<TAB>0 and a non-zero cap-line count). 2. A verification-only criterion (a close with no source diff) can be judged to a COMPLETE tier 2 within the configured cap: prove it by judging one such row end to end and recording the run directory, the iteration count actually used, and a non-empty items[] in the verdict. 3. The evaluator detects a wait that cannot fire instead of spending its budget on it: given a poll whose target log exists but can never contain the awaited marker, the run ends with a named reason (not a bare cap line) in bounded time, and the same fixture with a marker that DOES appear completes normally. 4. The recipe the repository documents for a verification-only close names no live-host probe (scheduler.db, a hosted API call, or the full suite) or the evaluator is bounded so that such a probe cannot consume the cap; whichever shape is chosen, docs/ records it next to the tier-2 cap guidance. 5. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS and bash scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK at the closing commit.","probe":"test -f .gitreins/history/2026-11-06/6cfe3061/verdict.json && jq -r '[.passed, (.items|length)]|@tsv' .gitreins/history/2026-11-06/6cfe3061/verdict.json && grep -c 'Iteration cap' .gitreins/history/2026-11-06/6cfe3061/verdict.json"}
 {"id":"CF-376","title":"The worker seat names a pair the router registry does not carry: worker_model deepseek-v4-flash@deepseek-foreman is absent from the registry CF-245 validates pins against","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-06T06:03:32Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T06:03:32Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"815ad62de001b1218e9148b4dbae5b909b4c65df94fdf22ae715a5ad5e7f71e4"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-06 by tick code-factory-2026-11-06-04-01-25, while closing CF-245.\n\nCF-245's guard (scheduler repo commit 572b111f: internal/config/pin_registry.go + pin_registry_test.go + loader.go wiring, 4 tests PASS on origin/main e46f2252) validates the LANE pin: the (model, provider) pair in fleet.toml's [[projects]] block. The WORKER seat of the same lane is not covered, and it currently names a pair the registry does not carry.\n\nEvidence, all re-runnable:\n  sqlite3 ~/.hermes/scheduler.db \"select name,model,provider,worker_model,worker_provider from projects where name='code-factory';\"\n    code-factory|deepseek-flash|deepseek-foreman|deepseek-v4-flash|deepseek-foreman\n  python3 - <<'PY' over task-router/registry.json (tables.models)\n    deepseek-foreman rows: deepseek-flash (enabled), deepseek-v4-pro (enabled), deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp (enabled but valid_to 2026-09-10)\n    deepseek-v4-flash rows: clinepass (enabled), commandcode (enabled) - never deepseek-foreman\n\nSo the tick seat (model/provider) is a registry pair that CF-245's validator would pass, while the worker seat (worker_model/worker_provider) is the NOT-IN-REGISTRY shape the same validator was built to name. The dispatch still worked: this tick's worker ran on deepseek-v4-flash@deepseek-foreman and committed (2ef9cfe, later discarded as a duplicate - see the CF-245 event), so the provider accepts the name as an alias while the registry - the artifact the pin validator and the scheduler's rating logic read - does not know it. Every worker dispatch on this lane therefore has a model_served that cannot be attributed from the registry, which is exactly the unmeasured-pin class CF-245 closed, one layer down.","acceptance_criteria":"1. One command re-measures the class fleet-wide: for every projects row carrying a non-empty worker_model AND worker_provider, resolve the pair against registry tables.models and print the count of rows naming an absent or disabled pair plus the names. Before the fix it names code-factory (and any sibling lane with the same drift); after the fix it prints 0.\n2. Either the worker seats are corrected to registry pairs in both stores that outrank the router (scheduler.db projects.worker_model/worker_provider and fleet.toml if the lane carries the mirror), or the registry gains an entry for the alias that states what actually serves it. The row records which shape was chosen, the file, and the exact edit - no silent third option.\n3. The guard covers the worker seat, not only the lane pin: a fixture project naming an absent or disabled worker pair produces the named warning, proven by a test in the scheduler repo that goes red when the worker-seat coverage is removed (the same red/green discipline pin_registry_test.go uses for the lane pin).\n4. The row states whether any worker dispatch produced an unattributable model_served: read state.db session_model_usage for this lane's worker sessions and report the served model, rather than asserting the seat from its label.","probe":null,"capability_tags":["config-validation","registry","worker-seat"]}
